Restrict request-body charset in startStandaloneServer to UTF-8/16/32 (CVE-2026-23897)

The standalone server accepted request bodies in any character encoding and
passed them to the JSON body parser. Per RFC 8259 JSON text must be UTF-8
(RFC 7159 additionally allows UTF-16/UTF-32); accepting other charsets allows
smuggling/mis-decoding of the request body. This backports the upstream fix
(commits d25a5bdc / e9d49d16, released in @apollo/server 4.13.0) by validating
the Content-Type charset in the express.json() verify callback and rejecting
unsupported encodings with HTTP 415. The charset is parsed with a small
self-contained helper so no new runtime dependency (and no lockfile change)
is required.

The published npm tarball ships sources under src/ (not the monorepo
packages/server/src/ layout) and does not include the __tests__ tree, so this
patch targets src/standalone/index.ts and omits the test-only hunk.

--- a/src/standalone/index.ts
+++ b/src/standalone/index.ts
@@ -29,6 +29,19 @@
   >;
 }

+// according to RFC8259, only UTF-8 is allowed in JSON text
+// (see https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8259#section-8.1)
+// RFC 7159 also specifies that JSON could be UTF-16 or UTF-32,
+// so we allow for that, too
+const validCharset = /^utf-(8|((16|32)(le|be)?))$/i;
+
+// Extract the charset parameter of a Content-Type header without pulling in an
+// extra runtime dependency (mirrors content-type.parse's charset lookup).
+function requestCharset(contentType: string | undefined): string {
+  const match = /;\s*charset\s*=\s*"?([^";]+)"?/i.exec(contentType ?? '');
+  return (match?.[1] ?? 'utf-8').trim();
+}
+
